/**
 * Fibonacci Number
 * Topic: Recursion (Binary) + Memoization
 *
 * CONSTRAINTS:
 * - n >= 0 (no negatives)
 * - Two base cases needed — fib(0)=0, fib(1)=1
 *   Missing either base case causes recursion into negatives!
 *
 * =====================================================================
 * APPROACH 1 — Naive Recursive (Binary Recursion)
 * =====================================================================
 * WHY: Direct translation of mathematical definition.
 *      fib(n) = fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)
 * HOW: Two base cases, two recursive calls per level.
 *      Forms a binary recursion tree — bushy and deep.
 *
 * PROBLEM: Overlapping subproblems!
 *      fib(3) computed twice, fib(2) computed three times for fib(5).
 *      Work explodes exponentially for large n.
 *
 * COMMON MISTAKE: Single base case if(n<=0) return 0
 *      fib(1) falls through to recursive case → fib(0)+fib(-1)
 *      Technically works but allows negative n into recursion — wrong!
 *      Always use two explicit base cases.
 *
 * TC: O(2^n) — roughly 2 calls per level, n levels deep
 * SC: O(n) — maximum stack depth at any moment = n (left branch)
 */
public int fibNaive(int n) {
    if (n == 0) return 0;
    if (n == 1) return 1;
    return fibNaive(n - 1) + fibNaive(n - 2);
}

/**
 * =====================================================================
 * APPROACH 2 — Memoized Recursive (Top Down DP)
 * =====================================================================
 * WHY: Naive approach recomputes same values millions of times.
 *      Memoization stores computed results — each value computed exactly once.
 *
 * HOW:
 * Step 1 — check base cases first
 * Step 2 — check memo before computing (if found, return directly)
 * Step 3 — not in memo → compute recursively
 * Step 4 — store result in memo before returning
 *
 * KEY INSIGHT:
 * - memo.containsKey(n) check MUST come after base cases.
 *   Base cases never need memo — they return directly.
 * - Always non-tail recursive — frame must wait to:
 *   (1) add fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)
 *   (2) store result in memo
 *   (3) return result
 * - This is the gateway concept to Dynamic Programming (DP).
 *   Overlapping subproblems + optimal substructure = DP candidate.
 *
 * COMMON MISTAKE: Forgetting to store result in memo before returning.
 *   Without memo.put(), same values get recomputed — defeats the purpose!
 *
 * Call as: fibMemo(5, new HashMap<>())
 *
 * TC: O(n) — each value from 0 to n computed exactly once
 * SC: O(n) — memo map stores n values + stack depth n
 */
public int fibMemo(int n, HashMap<Integer, Integer> memo) {
    if (n == 0) return 0;
    if (n == 1) return 1;
    if (memo.containsKey(n)) return memo.get(n);    // already computed, return directly
    int result = fibMemo(n - 1, memo) + fibMemo(n - 2, memo);  // compute
    memo.put(n, result);                             // store before returning
    return result;
}

/**
 * =====================================================================
 * APPROACH 3 — Iterative (Bottom Up)
 * =====================================================================
 * WHY: Best for production. No recursion, no memo map needed.
 *      Build answer from bottom up — fib(0), fib(1), fib(2)...fib(n)
 *
 * HOW: Track only previous two values — prev1 and prev2.
 *      Each iteration compute next = prev1 + prev2, slide window forward.
 *
 * TC: O(n) | SC: O(1) — no stack frames, no extra map
 */
public int fibIterative(int n) {
    if (n == 0) return 0;
    if (n == 1) return 1;
    int prev2 = 0, prev1 = 1;
    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
        int next = prev1 + prev2;
        prev2 = prev1;
        prev1 = next;
    }
    return prev1;
}
